                <h2>Pre-Setup: Confirm the Review Scope</h2>
<p>Before starting the review, clarify the following:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Review Period</strong>: Weekly is recommended; choose the past 7-14 days of data</li>
<li><strong>Account Level</strong>: Prioritize Campaign or Ad Set level filtering; avoid evaluating single Ad performance only</li>
<li><strong>Data Source</strong>: Export reports from Meta Ads Manager including CPM, CPC, CTR, CPA, and ROAS</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>Tip: For new ad accounts, accumulate at least 50-100 conversions before making creative-level judgments. Insufficient data leads to misjudgments.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Filter Creatives That "Can Run"</h2>
<p>This step aims to identify creatives with <strong>spend and impressions</strong> - ones that are actually distributing.</p>
<h3>Filter Criteria (any one applies):</h3>
<ol>
<li>Spend &gt; 50% of the account's daily average spend</li>
<li>Impressions &gt; 10,000</li>
<li>Reach &gt; 5,000</li>
</ol>
<h3>Procedure:</h3>
<ol>
<li>Export the past 14 days of data, sorted by Spend in descending order</li>
<li>Flag the top 30% of creatives by Spend</li>
<li>Check the Frequency of these creatives; exclude those showing "running" appearance due to over-delivery</li>
<li>Record the baseline CPM and CPC values for this group</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Qualification Standard</strong>: CPM within ±30% of category average, with Frequency &lt; 4</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If a creative's CPM is abnormally high (e.g., 2x the average), the system is already reducing its delivery frequency - strictly speaking, it doesn't "run well."</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Identify Creatives That "Can Sell"</h2>
<p>"Can sell" means the creative can complete your business objective. Different optimization goals require different evaluation criteria.</p>
<h3>Judgement by Optimization Goal:</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Optimization Goal</th>
<th>Core "Can Sell" Metric</th>
<th>Benchmark</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>App Install</td>
<td>CPA + D1 Retention</td>
<td>CPA &lt; 120% of target; D1 retention &gt; 25%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>E-commerce</td>
<td>ROAS + Conversion Rate</td>
<td>ROAS &gt; 1.5; conversion rate &gt; 1%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Lead Generation</td>
<td>CPA + Form Submission Rate</td>
<td>CPA &lt; target; submission rate &gt; 3%</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Procedure:</h3>
<ol>
<li>From the "Can Run" creative list, mark those meeting the table criteria</li>
<li>Calculate the CAC distribution for this group</li>
<li>Identify the Top 3 creatives with the best CPA and set up separate ad groups for observation</li>
<li>Document specific failure reasons for "Can Run but Can't Sell" creatives:</li>
<li>High CTR but low conversion → Landing page or product mismatch</li>
<li>High impressions but low clicks → First 3 seconds of creative not engaging enough</li>
<li>Fast spend but slow conversions → Audience too broad or bid too high</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Build Creative Classification Files</h2>
<p>The purpose of review is to guide the next round of delivery, requiring reusable evaluation standards.</p>
<h3>Creative Classification Template:</h3>
<pre><code>Creative ID: [Auto-generated]
Launch Date:
Optimization Goal:

Core Metrics:
- Spend:
- CPM:
- CTR:
- CPA/ROAS:

Rating Result:
□ S-Class: Can run and can sell; suitable for scaling
□ A-Class: Can run; moderate data; continue monitoring
□ B-Class: Can run but poor conversions; retest after optimization
□ C-Class: Can run but poor data; recommend pausing

Failure Analysis: [Brief description]
Optimization Suggestions: [Actionable improvement directions]
</code></pre>
<h3>Review Output Checklist:</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Creative data export completed (Date range: ___)</li>
<li>[ ] Number of "Can Run" creatives marked: ___</li>
<li>[ ] Number of "Can Sell" creatives identified: ___</li>
<li>[ ] S/A-Class creatives scheduled for scaling tests</li>
<li>[ ] B/C-Class creatives documented with optimization directions</li>
<li>[ ] Review conclusions synced to the creative team</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: What if a creative can run but has volatile data?</strong></p>
<p>Extend the observation period to 14 days and check for: ① Competitors ramping up simultaneously; ② Creative fatigue; ③ Audience overlap. If volatility is due to external factors, continue monitoring. If it's a creative issue, replace it promptly.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Should I immediately turn off creatives with no conversions?</strong></p>
<p>Check Spend and conversion time lag first. If a creative has run for 3+ days, spent over 2x the target CPA, and has zero conversions, consider pausing. However, if it's due to narrow audience causing insufficient exploration, lower the bid or broaden targeting before observing further.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Closing</h2>
<p>"Can run" and "can sell" are two different evaluation dimensions. The former tests whether a creative can pass platform review and get distributed by the algorithm. The latter tests whether a creative can drive users to complete the target action.</p>
<p>I recommend scheduling a fixed creative review session weekly. After 3-4 weeks of consistency, you'll build your own creative performance baseline, dramatically improving future decision-making efficiency.</p>
